Buerger's Disease
Cardiovascular
Claudication of hands/feet, Heavy tobacco use, Distal digital ischaemia/gangrene, Corkscrew collaterals on angiography

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CDV-009
Reason for Visit: Unremitting resting pain in the distal extremity and new-onset discoloration of a digit requiring urgent assessment for critical limb ischemia.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted severe, agonizing resting pain in the foot of a young, heavy smoker, with reports of a toe turning black. Urgent medical assessment was mandatory to evaluate for limb-threatening acute ischemia or progression of gangrene, as delaying until regular hours risked irreversible tissue loss and systemic sepsis.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: The pain in my toes is excruciating, it wakes me up, and now my second toe has turned completely black.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports progressive cramping pain in the arch of the foot over the last 6 months during walking (claudication). Over the last 48 hours, the pain has become an exquisite, unremitting ache at rest, severely disrupting sleep. Has noticed the distal aspect of the right second digit has become necrotic and cold. Patient is a heavy tobacco user (30 cigarettes/day for 15 years). Reports occasional tender, red cords on the lower legs previously.
Functional Status: Unable to weight-bear on the affected foot due to severe pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone, relies on manual labor for income; high risk of amputation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 92 | Temp: 37.2 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: In obvious distress, holding affected foot, smelling strongly of tobacco smoke.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals palpable and strong femoral and popliteal pulses bilaterally. However, dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses are completely absent on the right, and severely diminished on the left. The right second toe exhibits dry gangrene distal to the proximal interphalangeal joint. The surrounding skin is erythematous but cool to the touch. Capillary refill in the right foot is >5 seconds. No signs of systemic sepsis.
Environmental Observations: Multiple full ashtrays observed throughout the living area.
Medication Reconciliation: On no regular medications. Self-medicating heavily with over-the-counter NSAIDs with no effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Buerger's Disease - Status: Exacerbated (Critical limb ischemia with dry gangrene)
Differentials:

Peripheral Arterial Disease (Atherosclerotic) - Ruled out/unlikely due to young age, distal specific involvement with entirely normal proximal arterial pulses.

Diabetic Foot Ulcer/Ischemia - Ruled out/unlikely due to absence of diabetic history, lack of peripheral neuropathy, and intense ischemic pain (diabetic ulcers are often painless).

Raynaud's Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to fixed gangrenous changes and absent pulses, rather than transient vasospastic color changes.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Risk of major amputation)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The presentation of exquisitely painful distal digital ischemia and gangrene in a young, heavy smoker with preserved proximal pulses is the hallmark of Buerger's Disease (Thromboangiitis Obliterans). The objective clinical finding of absent distal pulses and established tissue necrosis confirms critical limb ischemia. This presentation mandates urgent after-hours medical evaluation to initiate potent analgesia, prevent secondary infection, and facilitate urgent vascular surgery referral to salvage the limb, strictly fulfilling the criteria for urgent intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered acute opioid analgesia (Endone 5mg) on-site for severe resting pain. Urgent referral to the hospital emergency department for vascular surgery consultation and conventional peripheral angiography (to visualize corkscrew collaterals).
Positive Behavioral Change: To halt the destruction of your blood vessels, absolute and immediate cessation of all tobacco and nicotine products is mandatory. Hang the affected foot over the edge of the bed to allow gravity to assist blood flow and reduce pain.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Hospital admission required.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed to ED immediately via private transport or ambulance. Call 000 if developing a high fever, red streaks spreading up the leg, or sudden, rapid worsening of the pain.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The survival of your toe and foot depends entirely on urgent hospital treatment and permanent smoking cessation.
Clinical Course: The chemicals in tobacco are causing your small blood vessels to clot and die. If you continue to smoke, you will inevitably lose fingers and toes to amputation.

